---
title: ModMox
blurb: A Magic rules engine in Godot, built to the Comprehensive Rules so a roguelike can let players rewrite their own cards, with an AI to play against.
tags: [game]
tools: [Godot 4.7.2, GDScript, GUT]
date: 2026-11-06
repo: https://github.com/BumbleBeing/ModMox
---

ModMox is going to be a roguelike RPG where you fight your way through a run with a
deck of Magic: the Gathering cards, and the cards you earn along the way can be
changed. A spell might lose a point of its cost, a creature might pick up flying, a
burn spell might stop targeting and hit everything instead. Right now the work is the
part that makes all of that possible: a rules engine for Magic, an AI that plays it,
and a screen to play on. The engine is written in GDScript as a self-contained Godot
addon that knows nothing about the game around it.

Twelve milestones in, the engine handles the pregame and London mulligans, every phase
and step of a turn, priority and the stack, lands and mana, casting with targets,
combat with first strike, double strike and trample, state-based actions, triggered
abilities, the layer system for static abilities, and replacement and prevention
effects. The current card pool is 41 cards from Alpha, from Grizzly Bears and
Lightning Bolt to Serra Angel and Shivan Dragon, and every one of them is plain data.
The thirteenth milestone is the GUI, which is far enough along that you can play an
encounter against the AI by clicking on things.

## Why follow the real rules

The obvious way to build a card game is to write each card as a little script. That
falls apart the moment players can modify cards, because a modified card is a card
nobody wrote. So the engine follows the actual structure of the Comprehensive Rules
from the start, priority, the stack, state-based actions, layers with timestamps and
replacement effects, even though the card pool is tiny. Scope gets cut by leaving out
whole mechanics, never by simplifying the architecture, and a rule that only matters
for one mechanic isn't written until a card needs it.

It follows current rules and current Oracle wording, even for Alpha cards. There's no
mana burn and no damage assignment order. If I ever want an old rule back for flavor,
it comes in as a named house rule rather than mixed into the core. Encounters are
always one on one, and there will never be planeswalkers.

## Cards are data

Card Definitions are Godot Resources assembled from a fixed library of primitives:
costs, effects, triggers, static abilities, replacement effects, Values, Filters and
Selections. Every number in an ability is a Value, so "deal 3 damage" can become "deal
damage equal to the number of Goblins you control" without new code. Every effect
reaches its targets through a Selection, so turning "target creature" into "each
creature" is a data change too. If a card can't be built from the primitives, the fix
is to add a primitive, not to script the card.

Rules classifications are worked out from a card's contents instead of being set by
hand. Whether an ability is a mana ability follows CR 605.1a, so a modification that
adds a target to `{T}: Add {R}` correctly turns it into an ability that uses the
stack. A card the player has earned is stored as an untouched base definition plus an
ordered list of Modifications, and the engine flattens it at the start of each
encounter into a printed card that simply is what it is.

## Decisions, replays and undo

The engine never waits for input. Whenever someone has a choice, it stops and returns
a Pending Decision listing the legal options, and it resumes when it gets an answer.
The GUI and the AI answer exactly the same decisions, which keeps everything testable
headless. Priority is answered with an index into the list of legal Actions, so an
illegal play can't even be expressed.

Every encounter is deterministic from its setup and seed, with separate random number
streams for the game and the AI so that tuning the AI never changes a shuffle. That
means a mid-encounter save is just the setup plus the list of answers, and loading
it replays them. Undo works the same way. The engine keeps a checkpoint at each point
where taking something back would be unfair, like a card being drawn, and undoing
restores the checkpoint and replays the few decisions since.

## An AI that doesn't cheat

The AI is its own addon that depends on the engine and is never referenced by it. It
holds no card names and no authored hints. To judge a play, it copies the game, tries
the play on the copy, and scores the board that comes out, so a card a player has
rewritten needs no new AI code. It never sees the real game either. It gets what its
player could legally see, plus a copy in which every hidden card has been dealt out
again at random, so it knows what could be in your hand and never what is.

On top of its heuristics sits Flat Monte Carlo, which plays the best few candidates
forward through the next turn on the same imagined worlds. Difficulty only sets how
much it searches. It never adds mistakes, and search budgets are counted in samples
instead of milliseconds, so the AI plays the same on any machine and after any reload.
The search doesn't buy much strength yet. Normal beat Easy 52.5% of the time over 200
games, short of the 55% I was aiming for, mostly because combat is planned the same
way at every level.

## The screen

Exactly one object in the GUI is allowed to touch the engine. The Encounter Presenter
runs the encounter, keeps the AI on a worker thread, and hands the screen a snapshot
of what the player may see after every change. The screen draws from the snapshot and
nothing else, so it can't leak a hidden card it was never given.

Cards are drawn from their characteristics, with frames baked from layers, so a card
that changes color mid-run gets the right frame without anyone drawing it. What a
click means is worked out in one place, and the glow that marks what you can choose is
drawn from the same answer. Activating abilities, paying mana, choosing targets and
all three combat decisions have their own inputs. The rest still go through a
temporary debug panel, and each one loses its debug form when its real input lands.

## How it's tested

Each milestone starts by agreeing on its scope and test scenarios, gets built
test-first with GUT, and isn't done until everything is green. There are around 870
tests across the engine, the AI and the GUI, run headless from one script, with a
check for leaked objects. To make sure the engine's tests actually pin the rules down,
I break the rules on purpose at the end of each engine milestone and count how many of
those mutations the suite catches. Milestone 10 caught 35 of 36. The AI gets a soak
test that plays whole seeded encounters against itself and checks that each one ends
and replays exactly, and tournaments between AI settings to measure which plays better.

## The architecture

- **Engine.** `addons/modmox_rules/`, built from `RefCounted` and `Resource` classes
  with no scene tree dependency, and no game content inside it.
- **AI.** `addons/modmox_ai/`, which reads only its player's view and looks ahead on
  re-dealt copies of the game.
- **GUI.** `ui/`, game-side, where a test fails if any file but the Encounter
  Presenter mentions the engine.
- **Content.** Card Definitions live game-side in `content/cards/` as `.tres` files,
  with a test that checks no two cards share a sub-resource.
- **Events.** Every change to the game goes through one pipeline: proposed, offered
  to replacement effects, applied, logged, then checked for triggers.
- **Layers.** Characteristics are always read through one query, and the layer engine
  works out every object on the battlefield at once, layer by layer, and caches the
  result until the next event.
- **Typing.** Static typing is enforced everywhere, with untyped declarations treated
  as compile errors, and a test fails if that setting is ever undone.
- **Decision records.** Twenty-two ADRs cover the design, alongside two glossaries
  and a milestone roadmap.

Next up is the stack, with arrows from spells to their targets and the first
animations, and then casting by dragging cards out of your hand. I wrote up the first
nine milestones in [A modular Magic engine](/blog/a-modular-magic-engine), and the AI
and the GUI in [ModMox gets an opponent](/blog/modmox-gets-an-opponent).
